## 4. Required acknowledgement, verbatim

Anything built on this data inherits this, because we inherit it:

> This research has made use of the NASA Exoplanet Archive, which is operated by the
> California Institute of Technology, under contract with the National Aeronautics and Space
> Administration under the Exoplanet Exploration Program.

The sky panels additionally use one VizieR catalogue (CDS, Strasbourg,
[doi:10.26093/cds/vizier](https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier)): **VI/42**, the IAU
constellation boundaries, which is what tells each host star which constellation it lies in.
VizieR's terms require the original catalogue author and publication to be cited explicitly
alongside VizieR itself — here, Roman N.G. (1987), *PASP* 99, 695.

All of the above is rendered for readers on the site's `/credits` page, generated from
`pipeline/rights.py` (and `web/credits.py`) so the page cannot fall behind this file.

## 5. Open question — the Cahoy grid

`data/cahoy_grid/` holds 305 CSV files obtained from `roman.ipac.caltech.edu`, **committed to
this repository**, and we have not located a terms-of-use page for them.

*Deriving* colours from published model output is uncontroversial — model results are facts.
*Redistributing the grid files themselves* is a separate act, and it is the one we have not
cleared. Two ways to close it, in order of preference:

1. Ask the Roman SSC (`roman-help@ipac.caltech.edu`) whether the archive carries terms.
2. Failing that, stop redistributing them: keep the CSVs out of the repository and fetch them
   at build time from the source, the way `planets.json` already works.

Until one of those happens, **do not include `data/cahoy_grid/` in any dataset deposit**
(Zenodo or otherwise). `scripts/release-data.sh` publishes only `planets.json`, so the
current release path is already clear of it.

